Disable each dialog input that no style read reaches

Twenty-nine inputs of the style dialog stay enabled in states where no
compiled read reaches their value. Custom shades hide the darker
borders box. The plain button effect hides the etched entry box, the
tool bar button effect and the custom alpha group. The rounding none
hides the eleven square boxes. A menubar with no shade hides the
shaded popup menu box. A user therefore sets a value that the style
throws away, while the label gives no hint.

The style draws the whole scrollbar groove for flat buttons, or for a
rounded scrollbar with no button. The other states hide the two groove
boxes. PM_DefaultFrameWidth still reads both boxes for a square scroll
view. That read gives a frame width, not a groove, so the gate leaves
it alone. The GTK scroll views hide the scrollbar background, unless
the scrollbar buttons are flat.

Give each input a gate in updateEnableStates(). Each gate carries the
condition of a style read site, or of the repair that qtcCheckConfig()
applies to a stored value. A gate clears no value. setOptions() still
writes what the input holds, so a configuration file survives a load
and an Apply unchanged.

The change sits on the branch work/dialog-state of the local tree, as commit 3 of 12.
A new merge request on system/qtcurve takes that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

